## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical knowledge of testing streaming systems, with emphasis on edge-case window behavior, fault tolerance, and end-to-end accuracy—skills essential for building production-grade data platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Windowing semantics and latency boundaries
- Idempotent and deduplicated alerting
- End-to-end streaming integration patterns
- Backpressure handling and burst tolerance
- Observability and feature drift detection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unit test for window aggregation correctness
async function testWindowAggregation() {
  const windowService = new WindowingService({
    windowSizeMs: 5000,
    slideIntervalMs: 1000
  });

  // Simulate late-arriving events within watermark tolerance
  await windowService.processEvents([
    { timestamp: 1000, value: 10 },
    { timestamp: 2000, value: 20 },
    { timestamp: 5500, value: 30 } // Late event within tolerance
  ]);

  const result = await windowService.getWindowResult();
  expect(result.sum).toBe(60);
  expect(result.count).toBe(3);
}

// Integration test for alert deduplication
async function testAlertDeduplication() {
  const alertService = new AlertService();

  // Simulate duplicate events from Kafka retry
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    await alertService.process({
      eventId: 'evt-123',
      anomalyScore: 0.95,
      timestamp: Date.now()
    });
  }

  const alerts = await alertService.getEmittedAlerts();
  expect(alerts.length).toBe(1); // Only one alert emitted
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design practical tests for eventual consistency in offline-first scenarios, including merge behavior, conflict resolution policy, and data integrity under CI.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first and eventual consistency
- Merge policies (last-writer-wins, deterministic merges, or CRDTs)
- Conflict scenarios and data integrity checks
- Deterministic test datasets and network partition simulations
- CI integration, test isolation, and seed data management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Jest-like test (pseudo)
describe('offline sync', () => {
  test('merges offline edits on reconnect using last-writer-wins', async () => {
    const server = new MockServer();
    const clientA = new Client(server, { offline: true });

    server.createNote({ id: 'n1', content: 'Hello' });

    // offline edit on client A
    clientA.createNoteOffline({ id: 'n1', content: 'Hello world' });

    // reconnect and sync
    await clientA.sync();

    // verify on server
    expect(server.getNote('n1').content).toBe('Hello world');
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend tests for concurrent offline edits on two devices?
- How would you validate no PII leakage during sync and how would you test rollback on failed sync?

